bodyText:
Every South Carolina tenant must, by statute:
- follow the building and housing code duties that fall on tenants and materially affect health and safety;
- keep the unit and the parts of the premises they use reasonably safe and clean;
- dispose of garbage and waste in a clean and safe way;
- keep plumbing fixtures reasonably clean;
- use the electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, appliances and elevators reasonably;
- not deliberately or negligently damage the premises, or knowingly let guests do so;
- behave, and make guests behave, so as not to disturb other tenants' peaceful enjoyment;
- follow the lease and enforceable rules;
- not unreasonably refuse lawful entry;
- not change the locks without permission;
- and use the unit only as a dwelling with no illegal activity unless otherwise agreed.

Breach of these duties is the basis for your remedies and deposit deductions.

South Carolina requires few disclosures in an ordinary residential lease:
- (1) the name and address of an owner or authorized agent for service of process and notices, in writing at or before the start of the tenancy;
- (2) if you rent more than four adjoining units and use different deposit standards, a statement of the standards posted or given before signing;
- (3) if you want the lease itself to be the nonpayment notice, the statutory "IF YOU DO NOT PAY YOUR RENT ON TIME" paragraph in conspicuous type;
- (4) any right to enter for scheduled periodic services must be conspicuously in the lease;
- (5) any appliance you are excluding from your maintenance duty must be specifically excluded;
- (6) a written notice of any meter conservation charge on the unit's utility account to each later tenant (if you don't give it, the tenant may deduct the charge from rent for up to half the lease term);
- (7) written or verbal instructions at move-in for testing the smoke detectors and replacing batteries (`smoke-detectors-sc`); and
- (8) if you are a South Carolina real estate licensee (broker, associate or property manager) renting property you own (a personal transaction), your license status in bold, underlined capital letters on the first page of the lease (`licensee-status-disclosure-sc`).

Federal lead-paint disclosure applies to pre-1978 housing. There is no state notice of whether the unit is covered by a rent or just-cause law (none exists), and no state form that must be the first page (apart from a licensee's own status statement).

A lease with an option to purchase a home of one to four units is different: it is covered by the Residential Property Condition Disclosure Act, so before it is signed (or as the contract provides) the owner must give the tenant-buyer the Real Estate Commission's disclosure statement, unless both parties agree in writing not to (see `edu-lease-option-disclosure-sc`).

A licensee renting in a personal transaction must also reveal license status at first substantive contact with a consumer and in any advertising or marketing, and must deposit trust funds from the rental in the licensee's personal trust account, not the brokerage firm's trust account unless the firm manages, lists or owns the property (S.C. Code Ann. § 40-57-135(F)).

In South Carolina:
- (1) a tenant must not conduct or permit illegal activity in the unit, and doing so is a ground to end the lease on the five-day track (on the cure question, see `edu-cure-and-eviction-grounds-sc`);
- (2) knowingly keeping or maintaining a dwelling or other place used for unlawful controlled-substance activity is unlawful: a civil fine of up to $1,000, or a felony (up to five years or $10,000) if charged and found to be knowing or intentional;
- (3) a building used for repeated unlawful possession or sale of controlled substances, prostitution or continuous breach of the peace can be enjoined and closed for a year as a nuisance, after the prosecutor gives the owner 10 days' written notice to abate (see `edu-nuisance-sc`).

No statute makes eviction for crime a landlord duty, lets third parties file the eviction, or addresses crime-free addenda; the library's illegal-activity terms rely on the Act.

South Carolina's association laws (the Homeowners Association Act and the Horizontal Property Act for condominiums) say little about tenants. No statute requires association approval of a lease, caps an approval or rental fee, lets the association collect rent from your tenant when you're behind on dues, or limits rental restrictions to later buyers. What the statutes do:
- a condominium's master deed must state any restrictions on leasing (amount and term);
- each co-owner must comply strictly with the bylaws, rules and covenants, and the association may sue for damages or an injunction;
- the association may enter a unit at reasonable hours to maintain common elements or make emergency repairs;
- unpaid assessments are a lien the association can foreclose, and during that foreclosure the owner must pay a reasonable rental, collected by a court-appointed receiver;
- and no association document or lease may bar a homeowner or tenant from displaying one portable United States flag respectfully.

Check your governing documents before you lease, and bind the tenant to them (`hoa-compliance`; approval: `association-approval-la`).

South Carolina gives tenants these statutory routes out of a lease before its term ends, none of which the lease can waive and none of which the lease must describe:
- (1) a protected tenant (a domestic-violence victim whose abuser is a co-tenant, documented by a protection order or conviction) may end their obligations by written notice within 60 days of the incident, without early-termination fees (`edu-dv-lease-termination-sc`);
- (2) if you fail to deliver possession, the tenant may end the lease on five days' written notice (`edu-possession-delay-sc`);
- (3) after a fire or casualty substantially impairing use, the tenant may vacate and give written notice within seven days (`edu-casualty-termination-sc`);
- (4) for your material breach or a health-and-safety violation, the 14-day notice to remedy or end the lease;
- (5) for an unlawful ouster or shutoff, or abuse of access, the tenant may end the lease and recover damages.

Servicemembers have the federal SCRA termination rights (`edu-servicemember-rights-sc`). No statute releases a tenant for fraud in the lease or forfeits rent for omitting a statement of these rights.
